1
00:00:01,420 --> 00:00:03,980
The next of the microcytic anemias

2
00:00:03,980 --> 00:00:06,140
is anemia of chronic disease.

3
00:00:06,140 --> 00:00:07,640
Now what is anemia of chronic disease?

4
00:00:07,640 --> 00:00:09,380
This is anemia that's associated

5
00:00:09,380 --> 00:00:13,000
usually with chronic inflammation or cancer.

6
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:14,660
It's the most common type of anemia

7
00:00:14,660 --> 00:00:16,520
in hospitalized patients,

8
00:00:16,520 --> 00:00:20,420
and the idea here is that if there is a chronic disease

9
00:00:20,420 --> 00:00:23,040
that results in chronic inflammation,

10
00:00:23,040 --> 00:00:26,700
then there would be an increase in acute phase reactants.

11
00:00:26,700 --> 00:00:30,060
One of those acute phase reactants is called hepcidin.

12
00:00:30,060 --> 00:00:33,420
And hepcidin, what it does is it sequesters iron

13
00:00:33,420 --> 00:00:36,400
within storage sites so that it cannot be used.

14
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:39,840
So hepcidin has the ability to lock iron

15
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:41,620
into its storage sites,

16
00:00:41,620 --> 00:00:45,060
prohibiting iron from being transferred to other cells.

17
00:00:45,060 --> 00:00:48,300
So this limits the transfer of iron from the macrophages

18
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:49,800
to the erythroid precursors.

19
00:00:49,800 --> 00:00:52,840
And if the erythroid precursors don't have access to iron,

20
00:00:52,840 --> 00:00:55,360
they're gonna have difficulty producing heme.

21
00:00:55,360 --> 00:00:58,680
And again, if heme is low, then hemoglobin will be low.

22
00:00:58,680 --> 00:01:00,360
And if hemoglobin is low,

23
00:01:00,360 --> 00:01:03,660
that's gonna result in a microcytic anemia.

24
00:01:03,660 --> 00:01:06,980
So that's one consequence of hepcidin.

25
00:01:06,980 --> 00:01:08,600
The second consequence of hepcidin

26
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:11,880
is that it can suppress erythropoietin production as well.

27
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:14,540
And again, if you suppress erythropoietin production,

28
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:17,880
that's going to decrease the drive on the bone marrow

29
00:01:17,880 --> 00:01:20,180
to actually produce more cells.

30
00:01:20,180 --> 00:01:22,700
Now, one of the things to remember

31
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:25,260
concerning this sort of sequence of events

32
00:01:25,260 --> 00:01:28,080
is that it actually makes sense what's going on.

33
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:31,980
Iron is an essential nutrient for bacteria.

34
00:01:31,980 --> 00:01:34,480
They require iron for their survival.

35
00:01:34,480 --> 00:01:37,460
And so when there's chronic inflammation,

36
00:01:37,460 --> 00:01:40,280
the body doesn't know that there's some sort of chronic disease,

37
00:01:40,280 --> 00:01:41,820
like an autoimmune disease,

38
00:01:41,820 --> 00:01:44,500
or that there's a cancer with a little bit of chronic inflammation

39
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:45,720
associated with it.

40
00:01:45,720 --> 00:01:49,040
Instead, the body thinks that there is actually chronic inflammation

41
00:01:49,040 --> 00:01:51,860
because there is some sort of microbe present.

42
00:01:51,860 --> 00:01:54,540
And so what the body does is it releases hepcidin

43
00:01:54,540 --> 00:01:59,540
to hide away the iron so that the iron is not available for the bacteria.

44
00:01:59,540 --> 00:02:03,700
And that, you know, theoretically would then result in lack of the ability

45
00:02:03,700 --> 00:02:07,460
of the bacteria to divide and to thrive.

46
00:02:07,460 --> 00:02:10,760
But obviously, in chronic disease, there is no infection.

47
00:02:10,760 --> 00:02:13,900
However, the effects of hepcidin are still felt.

48
00:02:13,900 --> 00:02:15,860
And so this is called anemia of chronic disease.

49
00:02:15,860 --> 00:02:20,860
Now, here I remind you that there's a decreased availability of iron.

50
00:02:20,860 --> 00:02:23,360
It's the same story as I've mentioned before.

51
00:02:23,360 --> 00:02:25,460
Decreased iron, decreased heme.

52
00:02:25,460 --> 00:02:27,660
Decreased heme, decreased hemoglobin.

53
00:02:27,660 --> 00:02:32,560
Decreased hemoglobin is going to result in a microcytic anemia.

54
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:34,760
Now, what are the lab findings going to be?

55
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:39,760
Well, the lab findings are going to be based on our understanding

56
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:42,360
of what is driving this disease process.

57
00:02:42,360 --> 00:02:45,360
So, for example, first of all, you're well aware of the fact

58
00:02:45,360 --> 00:02:53,360
that in anemia of chronic disease, there is going to be an inability to use storage iron.

59
00:02:53,360 --> 00:02:57,360
So, if the storage iron cannot be used, it's going to pile up.

60
00:02:57,360 --> 00:03:00,360
And as it piles up, there will be high stores of iron,

61
00:03:00,360 --> 00:03:04,360
and so the ferritin will be high because the stores of iron are high.

62
00:03:04,360 --> 00:03:07,360
And at the same time, any time the ferritin is high,

63
00:03:07,360 --> 00:03:10,860
the total iron binding capacity will go down.

64
00:03:10,860 --> 00:03:15,860
And that's because the number of transferrin molecules floating around in the blood will go down.

65
00:03:15,860 --> 00:03:20,860
And this is sort of the relationship that we've already explained in iron deficiency anemia.

66
00:03:20,860 --> 00:03:26,860
Another important feature or finding will be that the serum iron will be used.

67
00:03:26,860 --> 00:03:34,860
If the bone marrow cannot use the iron in the macrophages, it will begin to take the iron from the serum.

68
00:03:34,860 --> 00:03:38,860
And when it takes the iron from the serum, the serum iron will go down.

69
00:03:38,860 --> 00:03:43,860
And at the same time, percent saturation will also go down.

70
00:03:43,860 --> 00:03:49,860
So, the serum iron will go down, and the percent saturation will go down.

71
00:03:49,860 --> 00:03:53,860
And then finally, there will be an increase in free erythrocyte protoporphyrin.

72
00:03:53,860 --> 00:03:59,860
Again, that makes sense because you understand, now that we have studied a little bit of the biochemistry here,

73
00:03:59,860 --> 00:04:08,460
that heme is made of iron plus protoporphyrin.

74
00:04:08,460 --> 00:04:13,460
And the problem here is the accessibility of iron.

75
00:04:13,460 --> 00:04:16,460
However, there is no problem with protoporphyrin.

76
00:04:16,460 --> 00:04:22,460
And therefore, protoporphyrin will be produced, and some of it will be floating free in the red blood cell.

77
00:04:22,460 --> 00:04:24,460
It will not be attached to iron.

78
00:04:24,460 --> 00:04:28,460
And so, there will be an increase in free erythrocyte protoporphyrin.

79
00:04:28,460 --> 00:04:35,460
Now, let me just clarify one bit of confusion that can arise depending on which textbook you're reading.

80
00:04:35,460 --> 00:04:38,460
And that is that when you consider this sequence of events,

81
00:04:38,460 --> 00:04:43,460
it's very similar to the sequence of events that would occur with iron deficiency anemia.

82
00:04:43,460 --> 00:04:48,460
Remember that in iron deficiency anemia, the first phase was to have a normalcytic anemia.

83
00:04:48,460 --> 00:04:55,460
And then, eventually, as the iron deficiency becomes worse, the patient then gets a microcytic hypochromic anemia.

84
00:04:55,460 --> 00:04:58,460
Well, the exact same thing happens in anemia of chronic disease.

85
00:04:58,460 --> 00:05:04,460
So, otherwise stated, what I'm trying to say is that in the early phase of anemia of chronic disease,

86
00:05:04,460 --> 00:05:07,460
the patient first develops a normalcytic anemia.

87
00:05:07,460 --> 00:05:12,460
And then, as the anemia of chronic disease becomes severe, and I should say, in particular,

88
00:05:12,460 --> 00:05:19,460
as the iron deficiency becomes more severe, the patient can then go on to develop a microcytic anemia.

89
00:05:19,460 --> 00:05:23,460
And the reason I'm highlighting this point here is because if you look in textbooks,

90
00:05:23,460 --> 00:05:28,460
some textbooks put anemia of chronic disease as a microcytic anemia,

91
00:05:28,460 --> 00:05:31,460
and other textbooks classify it as a normalcytic anemia.

92
00:05:31,460 --> 00:05:33,460
And it can actually do both.

93
00:05:33,460 --> 00:05:37,460
And so, I just want you to understand that sequence of events that we've already covered

94
00:05:37,460 --> 00:05:40,460
when we talked about iron deficiency anemia.

95
00:05:40,460 --> 00:05:45,460
Now, what is the treatment of anemia of chronic disease?

96
00:05:45,460 --> 00:05:48,460
The treatment would be to address the underlying cause.

97
00:05:48,460 --> 00:05:53,460
So, if there's a chronic inflammatory state, for example, the patient has an autoimmune disease,

98
00:05:53,460 --> 00:05:57,460
then we'd want to address the autoimmune disease by decreasing the inflammation,

99
00:05:57,460 --> 00:06:00,460
will decrease the production of hepcidin.

100
00:06:00,460 --> 00:06:07,460
And by decreasing hepcidin, we'll then be able to free the iron that's present in the storage sites

101
00:06:07,460 --> 00:06:10,460
and make it available to the bone marrow.

102
00:06:10,460 --> 00:06:13,460
Another important treatment could be erythropoietin.

103
00:06:13,460 --> 00:06:18,460
Remember that in a subset of, remember that one of the effects of hepcidin

104
00:06:18,460 --> 00:06:21,460
is to actually decrease the production of erythropoietin.

105
00:06:21,460 --> 00:06:26,460
And giving exogenous erythropoietin is particularly useful in a subset of patients,

106
00:06:26,460 --> 00:06:28,460
especially in cancer patients.

107
00:06:28,460 --> 00:06:32,460
So that if patients have cancer and anemia of chronic disease associated with that,

108
00:06:32,460 --> 00:06:37,460
erythropoietin can be particularly helpful in those patients.

109
00:06:37,460 --> 00:06:44,460
The next of the microcytic anemias that I would like to discuss is called sideroblastic anemia.

110
00:06:44,460 --> 00:06:50,460
Now, sideroblastic anemia is going to be due to a defect in protoporphyrin synthesis.

111
00:06:50,460 --> 00:06:55,460
Again, go back and remind yourselves that heme, as I've stated many times already,

112
00:06:55,460 --> 00:07:01,460
heme is composed of iron plus protoporphyrin.

113
00:07:01,460 --> 00:07:08,460
And if there is a disorder in the ability to produce protoporphyrin,

114
00:07:08,460 --> 00:07:11,460
then low protoporphyrin would result in low heme,

115
00:07:11,460 --> 00:07:14,460
and low heme would result in low hemoglobin,

116
00:07:14,460 --> 00:07:18,460
and low hemoglobin would then result in a microcytic anemia.

117
00:07:18,460 --> 00:07:21,460
So that's the paradigm that we've established, and of course this follows that.

118
00:07:21,460 --> 00:07:23,460
So let's read what we have here.

119
00:07:23,460 --> 00:07:27,460
Sideroblastic anemia is due to defective protoporphyrin synthesis,

120
00:07:27,460 --> 00:07:33,460
and again that decreased protoporphyrin will eventually lead to a microcytic anemia.

121
00:07:33,460 --> 00:07:37,460
Now, in order to understand sideroblastic anemia,

122
00:07:37,460 --> 00:07:40,460
you then need to understand the biochemistry of protoporphyrin,

123
00:07:40,460 --> 00:07:43,460
and here's where things get a little bit complex.

124
00:07:43,460 --> 00:07:48,460
Recall that protoporphyrin is actually synthesized by about seven or eight chemical reactions.

125
00:07:48,460 --> 00:07:53,460
So there are a series of reactions that are important for the synthesis of protoporphyrin.

126
00:07:53,460 --> 00:07:59,460
And recall that some of these reactions occur within the cytoplasm of the cell,

127
00:07:59,460 --> 00:08:02,460
and some of these reactions occur within the mitochondria of the cell,

128
00:08:02,460 --> 00:08:07,460
and of course these reactions are occurring in the progenitor cells of the red blood cells,

129
00:08:07,460 --> 00:08:09,460
in the erythroblasts.

130
00:08:09,460 --> 00:08:12,460
I don't want to go through every one of these reactions,

131
00:08:12,460 --> 00:08:17,460
because that would get unnecessarily complex for the discussion that we want to have,

132
00:08:17,460 --> 00:08:23,460
but there are some very high yield reactions within this sequence that you do need to be familiar with,

133
00:08:23,460 --> 00:08:26,460
and they are highly tested on examinations,

134
00:08:26,460 --> 00:08:28,460
and of course they're clinically relevant,

135
00:08:28,460 --> 00:08:33,460
because they apply to our discussion of sideroblastic anemia.

136
00:08:33,460 --> 00:08:36,460
Let me go back and create a little bit of a whiteboard here,

137
00:08:36,460 --> 00:08:40,460
and remind you that the very first step in the production of protoporphyrin

138
00:08:40,460 --> 00:08:45,460
is that succinyl-CoA, which I'm going to just abbreviate here as this,

139
00:08:45,460 --> 00:08:50,460
is converted into aminolivolunic acid.

140
00:08:50,460 --> 00:08:51,460
That's the first step.

141
00:08:51,460 --> 00:08:58,460
And this particular reaction is catalyzed by an enzyme called aminolivolunic acid synthase,

142
00:08:58,460 --> 00:09:02,460
also called ALAS, A-L-A-S.

143
00:09:02,460 --> 00:09:09,460
Important to note that ALAS is the rate-limiting step in the production of protoporphyrin,

144
00:09:09,460 --> 00:09:11,460
so that is important to know.

145
00:09:11,460 --> 00:09:17,460
And the second important point is that vitamin B6 is a cofactor for this enzyme.

146
00:09:17,460 --> 00:09:20,460
So these are two important things to be aware of.

147
00:09:20,460 --> 00:09:28,460
The next step is going to be that ALA is going to be converted to porphylbolinogen,

148
00:09:28,460 --> 00:09:35,460
so I'm just going to call it porphylb, P-O-R-P-H-O-B, porphylbolinogen.

149
00:09:35,460 --> 00:09:42,460
And the important enzyme that catalyzes this reaction is called ALAD, A-L-A-D, dehydrogenase.

150
00:09:42,460 --> 00:09:44,460
So A-L-A-D.

151
00:09:44,460 --> 00:09:47,460
These are the first two reactions within this sequence.

152
00:09:47,460 --> 00:09:52,460
Now, the next series of reactions are, you know, there's a series of reactions that occur,

153
00:09:52,460 --> 00:09:56,460
so there's a few more reactions, and then you get to the very final reaction.

154
00:09:56,460 --> 00:10:00,460
And in the final reaction, you know, eventually you're going to generate from here,

155
00:10:00,460 --> 00:10:04,460
you're going to generate protoporphyrin, protoporphyrin.

156
00:10:04,460 --> 00:10:09,460
And in the final reaction, protoporphyrin is bound to iron, all right,

157
00:10:09,460 --> 00:10:12,460
and protoporphyrin and iron are going to yield heme.

158
00:10:12,460 --> 00:10:14,460
So that we are aware of.

159
00:10:14,460 --> 00:10:19,460
The joining of protoporphyrin to iron occurs by an enzyme called ferrokelatase.

160
00:10:19,460 --> 00:10:24,460
Ferrokelatase, ferrokelatase, F-E-R-R-O.

161
00:10:24,460 --> 00:10:28,460
Ferrokelatase is what catalyzes this reaction, number one.

162
00:10:28,460 --> 00:10:34,460
Number two, this reaction occurs within the mitochondria.

163
00:10:34,460 --> 00:10:37,460
So that's important to know, and you'll see why in a moment.

164
00:10:37,460 --> 00:10:41,460
Let's go back to our slides, and then, and hopefully this now becomes clear.

165
00:10:41,460 --> 00:10:45,460
Protoporphyrin is synthesized in a series of reactions.

166
00:10:45,460 --> 00:10:52,460
And important is that the final reaction attaches protoporphyrin to iron to make heme.

167
00:10:52,460 --> 00:10:54,460
And what's the name of the enzyme again?

168
00:10:54,460 --> 00:10:55,460
Ferrokelatase.

169
00:10:55,460 --> 00:10:57,460
And where does that occur?

170
00:10:57,460 --> 00:10:59,460
In the mitochondria.

171
00:10:59,460 --> 00:11:05,460
At the same time, while protoporphyrin is being generated by this series of reactions,

172
00:11:05,460 --> 00:11:08,460
iron is being transferred to the erythroid precursor.

173
00:11:08,460 --> 00:11:09,460
So here's the idea.

174
00:11:09,460 --> 00:11:12,460
You know, you've got this erythroblast, okay?

175
00:11:12,460 --> 00:11:15,460
And here's the nucleus of the erythroblast.

176
00:11:15,460 --> 00:11:19,460
And adjacent to the nucleus is usually where the mitochondria sit.

177
00:11:19,460 --> 00:11:22,460
And so what's happening in the mitochondria, or in the erythroblast,

178
00:11:22,460 --> 00:11:25,460
is that protoporphyrin is being prepared.

179
00:11:25,460 --> 00:11:31,460
And eventually, protoporphyrin's final reaction that generates protoporphyrin occurs within the mitochondria here.

180
00:11:31,460 --> 00:11:33,460
So protoporphyrin is produced in the mitochondria.

181
00:11:33,460 --> 00:11:38,460
At the same time, iron comes from the bone marrow macrophages,

182
00:11:38,460 --> 00:11:44,460
is transferred to the erythroblast, the cell that's producing the red blood cells,

183
00:11:44,460 --> 00:11:48,460
and then iron also gets transferred to the mitochondria.

184
00:11:48,460 --> 00:11:51,460
And when iron and protoporphyrin meet in the mitochondria,

185
00:11:51,460 --> 00:11:55,460
iron then gets bound to protoporphyrin, and that's what creates heme.

186
00:11:55,460 --> 00:11:56,460
All right?

187
00:11:56,460 --> 00:11:57,460
And so that's what I'm saying here.

188
00:11:57,460 --> 00:12:00,460
Is that iron is transferred to the erythroid precursor.

189
00:12:00,460 --> 00:12:03,460
It enters the mitochondria to form heme.

190
00:12:03,460 --> 00:12:06,460
Now let's pretend for a minute that protoporphyrin is deficient,

191
00:12:06,460 --> 00:12:08,460
so that we're not making protoporphyrin.

192
00:12:08,460 --> 00:12:10,460
If we're not making appropriate protoporphyrin,

193
00:12:10,460 --> 00:12:14,460
iron will enter into the mitochondria and will be trapped there.

194
00:12:14,460 --> 00:12:16,460
It has no way of getting out.

195
00:12:16,460 --> 00:12:18,460
And when iron gets trapped within the mitochondria,

196
00:12:18,460 --> 00:12:21,460
it's going to pile up, because iron is going to continue to come in,

197
00:12:21,460 --> 00:12:25,460
so more iron and more iron and more iron will come into the mitochondria.

198
00:12:25,460 --> 00:12:29,460
And mitochondria are generally present all around the nucleus,

199
00:12:29,460 --> 00:12:31,460
so these are going to be mitochondria,

200
00:12:31,460 --> 00:12:34,460
and each of them is going to become loaded with iron.

201
00:12:34,460 --> 00:12:37,460
And as they become loaded with iron,

202
00:12:37,460 --> 00:12:42,460
that's going to create a ring of iron-loaded mitochondria

203
00:12:42,460 --> 00:12:44,460
around the nucleus of the cell.

204
00:12:44,460 --> 00:12:49,460
And that creates the classic cell that's seen within sideroblastic anemia,

205
00:12:49,460 --> 00:12:52,460
which is called a ringed sideroblast.

206
00:12:52,460 --> 00:12:55,460
And so that's what I'm saying here in this particular slide.

207
00:12:55,460 --> 00:12:56,460
Note what I say.

208
00:12:56,460 --> 00:13:01,460
Iron-laden mitochondria form a ring around the nucleus of erythroid precursors,

209
00:13:01,460 --> 00:13:03,460
and these are called ringed sideroblasts.

210
00:13:03,460 --> 00:13:05,460
So here's a classic image.

211
00:13:05,460 --> 00:13:06,460
This is a bone marrow biopsy.

212
00:13:06,460 --> 00:13:08,460
Of course, we would see this in the bone marrow.

213
00:13:08,460 --> 00:13:10,460
This is where the cells are being produced.

214
00:13:10,460 --> 00:13:13,460
And we can clearly see, see this blue color here?

215
00:13:13,460 --> 00:13:14,460
This represents iron.

216
00:13:14,460 --> 00:13:17,460
This is a special stain called the Prussian blue stain,

217
00:13:17,460 --> 00:13:20,460
and the Prussian blue stain is what marks iron.

218
00:13:20,460 --> 00:13:23,460
Now clearly, in this particular case, we see that there is iron

219
00:13:23,460 --> 00:13:26,460
that's accumulating in a ring around this cell.

220
00:13:26,460 --> 00:13:29,460
I'm going to clear this for you so that you can see it.

221
00:13:29,460 --> 00:13:34,460
Iron is accumulating in a ring around the nucleus of this particular cell.

222
00:13:34,460 --> 00:13:36,460
That's called a ringed sideroblast.

223
00:13:36,460 --> 00:13:38,460
Here's another example of a ringed sideroblast.

224
00:13:38,460 --> 00:13:42,460
And it indicates a sideroblastic anemia.

225
00:13:42,460 --> 00:13:45,460
One of the classic questions that examiners like to ask,

226
00:13:45,460 --> 00:13:48,460
they like to show you this picture, and they like to ask you,

227
00:13:48,460 --> 00:13:51,460
where is the iron located?

228
00:13:51,460 --> 00:13:53,460
I just gave away the answer.

229
00:13:53,460 --> 00:13:57,460
The answer is that the iron is present in mitochondria.

230
00:13:57,460 --> 00:13:59,460
That's high yield to be aware of.

231
00:13:59,460 --> 00:14:02,460
The iron is present within mitochondria.

232
00:14:02,460 --> 00:14:07,460
Sideroblastic anemia can actually be divided into congenital or acquired.

233
00:14:07,460 --> 00:14:13,460
It is important to know that the most common form of congenital sideroblastic anemia

234
00:14:13,460 --> 00:14:16,460
is due to a defect in ALAS.

235
00:14:16,460 --> 00:14:19,460
Remember that ALAS is the rate-limiting step.

236
00:14:19,460 --> 00:14:25,460
It is the step that catalyzes succinyl CoA to become amino levallunic acid.

237
00:14:25,460 --> 00:14:28,460
And that is the key rate-limiting step,

238
00:14:28,460 --> 00:14:32,460
and a congenital defect of this enzyme is the most common cause

239
00:14:32,460 --> 00:14:35,460
of congenital sideroblastic anemia.

240
00:14:35,460 --> 00:14:40,460
Again, important to be aware of for the purposes of examinations.

241
00:14:40,460 --> 00:14:44,460
As far as acquired sideroblastic anemia is concerned,

242
00:14:44,460 --> 00:14:46,460
these are also very high yield.

243
00:14:46,460 --> 00:14:50,460
What are the common causes of acquired sideroblastic anemia?

244
00:14:50,460 --> 00:14:54,460
So one important cause of acquired sideroblastic anemia is alcoholism.

245
00:14:54,460 --> 00:14:57,460
Alcohol is a mitochondrial poison.

246
00:14:57,460 --> 00:15:00,460
It damages the production of protoporphyrin,

247
00:15:00,460 --> 00:15:03,460
and therefore it can lead to a sideroblastic anemia.

248
00:15:03,460 --> 00:15:07,460
Another important cause of sideroblastic anemia is lead poisoning.

249
00:15:07,460 --> 00:15:10,460
Lead has the ability to denature enzymes,

250
00:15:10,460 --> 00:15:18,460
and two of the enzymes that lead can denature are ALAD and ferrokelatase.

251
00:15:18,460 --> 00:15:20,460
So a couple things.

252
00:15:20,460 --> 00:15:23,460
Number one, if ALAD is denatured,

253
00:15:23,460 --> 00:15:26,460
remember that ALAD was the second reaction in the production of protoporphyrin.

254
00:15:26,460 --> 00:15:32,460
If ALAD is denatured, that would then decrease our ability to produce protoporphyrin,

255
00:15:32,460 --> 00:15:34,460
and if protoporphyrin is not available,

256
00:15:34,460 --> 00:15:36,460
iron will become trapped in the mitochondria.

257
00:15:36,460 --> 00:15:39,460
And similarly, if ferrokelatase is not available,

258
00:15:39,460 --> 00:15:43,460
ferrokelatase is the name of the enzyme that links protoporphyrin with iron.

259
00:15:43,460 --> 00:15:47,460
And so if iron can't get linked to protoporphyrin to make heme,

260
00:15:47,460 --> 00:15:51,460
to eventually become hemoglobin, to then leave the cell,

261
00:15:51,460 --> 00:15:54,460
iron will get trapped in the mitochondria as well.

262
00:15:54,460 --> 00:15:58,460
And so lead poisoning is another cause of a sideroblastic anemia.

263
00:15:58,460 --> 00:16:02,460
And the third acquired cause of sideroblastic anemia is vitamin B6 deficiency.

264
00:16:02,460 --> 00:16:09,460
Now remember that the rate-limiting enzyme in this biochemical pathway was called ALAS,

265
00:16:09,460 --> 00:16:11,460
ALAS, A-L-A-S.

266
00:16:11,460 --> 00:16:15,460
And ALAS requires vitamin B6 as a cofactor.

267
00:16:15,460 --> 00:16:18,460
And so if there is a deficiency of vitamin B6,

268
00:16:18,460 --> 00:16:21,460
the ALAS enzyme will not function properly.

269
00:16:21,460 --> 00:16:25,460
That's the rate-limiting step for the production of protoporphyrin,

270
00:16:25,460 --> 00:16:30,460
and therefore the patients will get a sideroblastic anemia.

271
00:16:30,460 --> 00:16:36,460
Now what's the common circumstance in which patients get vitamin B6 deficiency?

272
00:16:36,460 --> 00:16:40,460
Well, the important common circumstance would be isoniazid treatment.

273
00:16:40,460 --> 00:16:42,460
And that's a little pharmacology tie-in.

274
00:16:42,460 --> 00:16:46,460
So that patients who are on isoniazid therapy,

275
00:16:46,460 --> 00:16:48,460
they can become vitamin B6 deficient,

276
00:16:48,460 --> 00:16:52,460
and can develop, as a consequence, a sideroblastic anemia.

277
00:16:52,460 --> 00:16:56,460
Now what are the laboratory findings going to be in a sideroblastic anemia?

278
00:16:56,460 --> 00:17:00,460
Well, generally speaking, the way I summarize the laboratory findings

279
00:17:00,460 --> 00:17:04,460
is that the patients, they get an iron overloaded state.

280
00:17:04,460 --> 00:17:10,460
The idea here is that pretend for a moment that this is the erythroblast.

281
00:17:10,460 --> 00:17:13,460
This is the erythroid progenitor cell.

282
00:17:13,460 --> 00:17:15,460
It's the cell that's making red blood cells.

283
00:17:15,460 --> 00:17:18,460
So what's happening is that iron is coming into the cell.

284
00:17:18,460 --> 00:17:19,460
Here's the nucleus.

285
00:17:19,460 --> 00:17:21,460
And iron begins to build up in the mitochondria,

286
00:17:21,460 --> 00:17:24,460
and of course more iron, more iron comes in,

287
00:17:24,460 --> 00:17:27,460
until finally there's just a ton of iron in this cell.

288
00:17:27,460 --> 00:17:32,460
So the cell is getting loaded and loaded and loaded with iron.

289
00:17:32,460 --> 00:17:37,460
Until eventually, iron, because it has the ability to create free radicals,

290
00:17:37,460 --> 00:17:40,460
eventually iron will cause the cell to get damaged and die.

291
00:17:40,460 --> 00:17:45,460
And so when the free radicals are generated by all of this excess iron,

292
00:17:45,460 --> 00:17:46,460
this cell will die.

293
00:17:46,460 --> 00:17:50,460
When this cell dies, a couple things are going to happen.

294
00:17:50,460 --> 00:17:52,460
But most importantly, the iron is going to leak out.

295
00:17:52,460 --> 00:17:58,460
And when the iron leaks out, the bone marrow macrophages will eat that iron.

296
00:17:58,460 --> 00:18:02,460
So there'll be high stores of iron in the bone marrow macrophages.

297
00:18:02,460 --> 00:18:05,460
And some of that iron could leak out into the blood,

298
00:18:05,460 --> 00:18:08,460
then causing an increase in serum iron.

299
00:18:08,460 --> 00:18:10,460
And so that's the way I sort of think about it.

300
00:18:10,460 --> 00:18:12,460
And that's how the labs sort of play out.

301
00:18:12,460 --> 00:18:14,460
These patients have a high ferritin,

302
00:18:14,460 --> 00:18:18,460
because the bone marrow macrophages are going to eat all of that stored iron,

303
00:18:18,460 --> 00:18:23,460
are going to store all of that iron that comes off from the dead erythroid precursors.

304
00:18:23,460 --> 00:18:27,460
And because the ferritin is high, the TIBC obviously would be low.

305
00:18:27,460 --> 00:18:30,460
And then at the same time, the serum iron is going to be high.

306
00:18:30,460 --> 00:18:35,460
And because the serum iron is high, the percent saturation would also be high.

307
00:18:35,460 --> 00:18:38,460
And so I've already explained this, and I think this makes sense.

308
00:18:38,460 --> 00:18:43,460
But basically the key summary sort of phrase would be iron overloaded state.

309
00:18:43,460 --> 00:18:47,460
And by the way, if you're curious, when patients have hemochromatosis,

310
00:18:47,460 --> 00:18:50,460
which is also a disease in which they have an iron overloaded state,

311
00:18:50,460 --> 00:18:53,460
they have very similar laboratory findings.

312
00:18:53,460 --> 00:18:58,460
So hemochromatosis and sideroblastic anemia share the same laboratory findings

313
00:18:58,460 --> 00:19:02,460
as far as iron state or status is concerned.

